/-! # Étale, affine pro-étale and weakly étale morphisms

Source: literature/blueprint/main-20261015/blueprint/sec-proetale-coefficients/sk-def-pe-morphisms/
`det-pe-morphisms-etale-comparison.md` (blueprint: det-pe-morphisms-etale-comparison).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- The four comparisons of blueprint node det-pe-morphisms-etale-comparison.
Source: `det-pe-morphisms-etale-comparison.md`, clauses 1--4; Stacks Tags 094X,
097N, 025G, 0818, and 01U5 (blueprint: det-pe-morphisms-etale-comparison).

Proof:
1. For affine opens V in U and T in X with u(V) contained in T,
   `lem-weil-pbc-open-fibre-product` and `lem-weil-pbc-fibre-product-affine-spectra`
   identify V ×_T V with the open p₁⁻¹(V) ∩ p₂⁻¹(V) in U ×_X U and with
   Spec(B ⊗_R B). The inverse image of this open under the diagonal is V.
   The restricted diagonal is therefore Spec of multiplication b ⊗ c ↦ bc.
2. `lem-weil-pbc-flat-morphism-affine-criterion` and
   `lem-weil-pbc-flat-ring-map-iff-flat-affine` identify the two flatness conditions
   on each chart. The opens above need only cover the image of the diagonal:
   restriction preserves local rings there. The definition
   `def-weil-weakly-etale-morphism` now gives clause 4 in both directions.
3. By `def-weil-etale-morphism` and `def-weil-unramified-morphism`, an étale
   morphism is flat with open-immersion diagonal. An open immersion induces
   local-ring isomorphisms, which are flat, giving clause 1.
4. By `lem-weil-pbc-ind-etale-stability`, an ind-étale ring map and its tensor
   multiplication are flat. Step 1 and the affine flatness equivalence give
   flatness of the spectrum map and its entire diagonal, proving clause 2.
5. For clause 3, `lem-weil-pbc-lfp-affine-opens-finite-presentation` presents B
   over R with generators x₁,...,xₙ. The multiplication kernel in B ⊗_R B is
   generated by xᵢ ⊗ 1 - 1 ⊗ xᵢ: modulo these relations the map b ↦ b ⊗ 1
   is inverse to multiplication. This is a finite presentation with no algebra
   generators, including when n = 0 (`def-weil-pbc-finitely-presented-algebra`).
6. The diagonal charts of step 1 and the finite presentations of step 5 make
   the diagonal locally of finite presentation. It is a monomorphism, since
   either projection composed with it is the identity, and is flat by weak
   étaleness. `lem-pe-flat-lfp-monomorphism-open` makes it an open immersion.
7. The finite presentations also give local finite type. The open diagonal
   gives unramifiedness; flatness and local finite presentation then give
   étaleness by `def-weil-etale-morphism`, proving clause 3.
-/
theorem etale_indEtale_weaklyEtale_comparison :
    (∀ {U X : Scheme.{u}} (u : U ⟶ X),
      Etale u → WeaklyEtale u) ∧
    (∀ (R B : Type u) [CommRing R] [CommRing B] [Algebra R B],
      Algebra.IndEtale R B →
        WeaklyEtale
          (Spec.map (CommRingCat.ofHom (algebraMap R B)))) ∧
    (∀ {U X : Scheme.{u}} (u : U ⟶ X),
      WeaklyEtale u → LocallyOfFinitePresentation u → Etale u) ∧
    (∀ {U X : Scheme.{u}} (u : U ⟶ X),
      WeaklyEtale u ↔
        ∀ (T : X.Opens) (_hT : IsAffineOpen T) (V : U.Opens) (_hV : IsAffineOpen V)
          (e : V ≤ u ⁻¹ᵁ T),
          (u.appLE T V e).hom.Flat ∧
            (fun (_a : Algebra Γ(X, T) Γ(U, V)) ↦
              RingHom.Flat (Algebra.TensorProduct.lmul' Γ(X, T) (S := Γ(U, V))).toRingHom)
              (u.appLE T V e).hom.toAlgebra) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
